1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Today I'm going to be talking about word embeddings, which I think are one of the coolest things you can do with machine learning right now.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
And to explain why I think that, I'm just going to jump right in and give you an example of something you can do with word embeddings.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
So a few months ago I set up a program that just downloads tweets as people write tweets on Twitter and saves them to a file.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,000
And after running this program for about a month or two, I had collected a massive file with over five gigabytes of tweets after I compressed it.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
So I got a massive amount of data and it's just raw data that people typed on the internet.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
After getting all this data, I fed it directly into a word embedding algorithm,

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
which was able to figure out tons of relationships between words just from the raw things that people happen to type on the internet.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
So for example, if I put in a color, it can tell me a bunch of other colors.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
It never actually knew the idea of color going in, it didn't really know anything, and it pieced together that all these words are related.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
You can see I can put in other things like a kind of food and I get other kinds of food out.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:09,000
And I'll actually have a link in the description so that you can try this out yourself just to see how well it really learned the relationships between words.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
So you're probably wondering how this actually worked because it's kind of baffling.

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
All I gave it was things that people typed on the internet.

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
I didn't give it a dictionary, I didn't give it a list of synonyms.

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
I mean, I chose English, but I could have chosen any language and it still would have been just as successful.

16
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
So it's pretty impressive that it was able to do this just from raw text.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:39,000
And in this video, I really want to explain why this works and how it works and just I think it's super cool, so I want to share it with you.

18
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
So pretty much every word embedding algorithm uses the idea of context.

19
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
And to show you what I mean, I'm just going to give you a really simple example.

20
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
So here is an example of a sentence where there's a word missing.

21
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
So it says, I painted the bench blank and we're expected to fill in the blank.

22
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
So the obvious thing here is to put a color, right?

23
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
I painted the bench red, I painted the bench green, something like that.

24
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
And already we can see that, you know, if a word can show up in this context, it's likely to be a color.

25
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
But unfortunately, that's not always true.

26
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
You could also say I painted the bench today.

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Today is not a color, but the main takeaway is that context is really kind of closely related to meaning.

28
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
So that was an example where multiple different words could go into the same context

29
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
and we presume that those words are somehow related, at least a lot of them are.

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
But there's another way that context can help us and that's if two words happen to always appear in the same context at once.

31
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
So here are three different sentences that will help us understand this idea.

32
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
In the first sentence, I actually have two examples.

33
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Donald and Trump are likely to appear together because one's the first name of a person

34
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
and one's the last name of that same person.

35
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
So those words are closely related.

36
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
We also have United States, which is kind of just one logical word broken up into two smaller words.

37
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
So United and States are likely to appear together.

38
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And then in the second and third example, we have a joke and laugh are kind of related words.

39
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
You laugh at a joke.

40
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
So they're also likely to appear in the same context.

41
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Now, there's one subtle thing that I'd like to point out in this example,

42
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
which is that laugh and laughed might be different words.

43
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Like laugh is the present tense and laughed is the past tense.

44
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
And likewise, we could think about joke versus jokes, like one is plural, one is singular.

45
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
These are different forms of the same word.

46
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
And ideally, since we knew nothing about English going in,

47
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
our algorithm, our word embedding is going to have to learn that different forms of the same word are related.

48
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
It has to learn that laughed is somehow related to laugh.

49
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,000
And what you can see is, you know, these examples give you an idea of how the model might be able to do that

50
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
because you can see laughed appears with the word joke in the second sentence

51
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
and laugh appears with the word joke in the third sentence.

52
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
So ideally, the word embedding would figure out then that laugh and laughter related

53
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
since they're both related to joke.

54
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
So that's kind of where a word embedding gets its knowledge from.

55
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
It learns things via context.

56
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
It sees, you know, what words occur near other words.

57
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
But what does a word embedding actually do?

58
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
I still have to kind of formalize what we're after.

59
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
So in one sentence, a word embedding just converts words into vectors.

60
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
So you might give in a word like hamburger and you would get out a list of, say, 64 numbers.

61
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
And those numbers would describe the word.

62
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
And for word embedding to be good, we kind of require that the vectors carry some meaning.

63
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
So if I put in hamburger and cheeseburger into my model,

64
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
I want those vectors to be very close to each other because they're very related words.

65
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Whereas if I put in something else like Ferrari, like a kind of car, totally unrelated to hamburger,

66
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
I want the vector for Ferrari to be far away from the vector for hamburger.

67
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
And of course, all these distances are relative,

68
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
but you can kind of see what I mean that we want the closeness of these vectors

69
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
to resemble the closeness of the words that they represent.

70
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And in addition to this kind of idea of closeness,

71
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
we might also want there to be even more structure.

72
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
For example, if I do the vector for man minus the vector for woman,

73
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
and to subtract vectors, we just subtract each number from the corresponding number of the other vector.

74
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
So if I take the vector for man and I subtract the vector for woman,

75
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
I want that to somehow represent the difference between male and female.

76
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
And then if I add that vector to the vector for queen,

77
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
I want it to give me out something very, very close to the vector for king.

78
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
So I want these vectors to be related,

79
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
and I want the differences between vectors to also carry some meaning.

80
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
And I might add other constraints,

81
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
but the idea is I just want to encode as much meaning as I can into the vectors.

82
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
So how are we actually going to do that?

83
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:08,000
How are we going to solve for vectors for all of the words that ever appear on Twitter?

84
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
How are we going to produce the set of vectors that work?

85
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
So the first approach I'm going to be talking about is known as word-to-vec.

86
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
And it's probably the most famous kind of word embedding,

87
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
because it was the first word embedding to get the kind of impressive results

88
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
that state-of-the-art word embeddings get today.

89
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
So essentially word-to-vec is just a really simple neural network.

90
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
And if you've already seen my other videos on neural networks,

91
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
you might actually already be able to implement word-to-vec,

92
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
but I'm just going to try to describe it here on a high level to give you an idea of how it works.

93
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
So here's a really simple picture of what a word-to-vec neural network looks like.

94
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
You essentially feed in a word and it produces in the middle,

95
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
it produces a small vector, which is a word embedding,

96
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
and then it produces its output something like a context.

97
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
And to describe what this is in a little more detail,

98
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:05,000
I'm actually going to give an example of something we might ask a word-to-vec neural network to do.

99
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
What I've done is I've picked out a random tweet from my corpus

100
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
and I picked out a random word from within that tweet.

101
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
In this case, it was yellow.

102
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:18,000
And I'm going to feed as input the word yellow to the word-to-vec neural network.

103
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,000
And I'm going to try to get it as output to give me all the other words that were in the tweet.

104
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
So the word-to-vec neural network, in this case,

105
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,000
is just trying to predict context words from a word.

106
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:35,000
So how exactly is it that I feed in the word yellow and I get out all these context words?

107
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
How do I represent that for the neural network?

108
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Well, basically the network has a different input neuron for each different word.

109
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
So I take the neuron for whatever word I want to feed in

110
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
and I'll set that neuron to one and I'll set all the other neurons to zero.

111
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Then the neural network will just use regular neural network stuff to produce a small vector.

112
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Basically, that's just a hidden layer with 64 neurons.

113
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
And then using more neural network magic,

114
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
I'll produce back out a vector maybe with 100,000 components

115
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
and each neuron and that output vector corresponds to a word as well.

116
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
And I want every neuron to be set that is in the context.

117
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:18,000
And I want every neuron that wasn't in the context not to be set.

118
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
So why does this work?

119
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Why do we expect that the middle layer, when a word gets turned into a small vector,

120
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
why do we expect that small vector to actually be meaningful?

121
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Well, the answer is that that small vector is all the network has to figure out the context.

122
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
So it goes right from that small vector to the context.

123
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:43,000
So if two words have very similar contexts, it's really helpful for the neural network

124
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
if the small vector is similar for those two words

125
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:52,000
because if it produces a similar output, a similar vector makes sense.

126
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:57,000
So essentially, this model is just forcing the middle layer of the neural network

127
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:03,000
to correspond to meaning, you know, close words, words with similar contexts

128
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
will have close vectors in the middle of the network

129
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
just because that's what's easiest for the network to do.

130
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,000
So that's kind of just a really general overview of how word2vec works

131
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
and there's a lot more to it.

132
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
So I'll have a link to the actual original word2vec paper in the description

133
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
if you want to read more about it.

134
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
So besides word2vec, there are a bunch of other ways to generate word embeddings

135
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
and the majority of them are based on this thing called the co-occurrence matrix.

136
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
So here's a really simple example of what this might look like.

137
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Basically, both the rows and the columns correspond to different words

138
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:42,000
and the entry at any given point in the matrix just counts how many times

139
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
those two words happen in the same context.

140
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,000
So you can imagine how we might generate this thing.

141
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:52,000
For example, with Twitter data, we might just loop through all the tweets,

142
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
go through all the words and all those tweets,

143
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
and for every time two words occur in the same tweet,

144
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
we just add one to that entry in this matrix.

145
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Now, different methods will use this matrix in different ways,

146
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
but pretty much all of the methods utilize some amount of linear algebra.

147
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
So I'm going to be just talking about matrices and matrix multiplications,

148
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
dot products, things like that, and if you don't know linear algebra,

149
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
you're not really going to get much from this,

150
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
but that's why I kind of left it at the end of the video

151
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
because some people will get something out of this,

152
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
and I think it's somewhat interesting.

153
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
So probably the simplest approach to generating word embeddings

154
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:34,000
with a co-occurrence matrix is to just decompose the co-occurrence matrix

155
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
into the product of two much smaller matrices.

156
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
So I've drawn out the picture here.

157
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
You can basically see that I get this massive square matrix,

158
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
which is our co-occurrence matrix,

159
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:50,000
by multiplying a tall, skinny matrix and a short, wide matrix.

160
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
You think about how many entries are in the big matrix.

161
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
There's 100,000 squared.

162
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
That's a lot more information than is stored on the right side of this equation,

163
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
which is two relatively small matrices multiplied together.

164
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:07,000
So by decomposing this big co-occurrence matrix into these smaller ones,

165
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
we're clearly compressing some information,

166
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and in doing so, hopefully we have to extract a lot of meaning from the matrix

167
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
so that we can do that compression,

168
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:21,000
and that should allow us to at least generate decent embeddings.

169
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
Once we have this matrix decomposition,

170
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
which I haven't described exactly how we might find this yet,

171
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
but you could imagine there's plenty of methods in linear algebra

172
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
to decompose a matrix like singular value decomposition,

173
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
or you could use gradient descent or something like that.

174
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
But once you have this matrix decomposition,

175
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
we actually get word vectors pretty much for free.

176
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,000
For example, in the big co-occurrence matrix,

177
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
each row and each column corresponds to a word.

178
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
So if I go into this tall, skinny matrix,

179
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and I grab the nth entry, the entry for a certain word,

180
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
that is going to give me a vector, which is pretty small,

181
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
in this case 64 components.

182
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
And I can call that a word embedding for that word.

183
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
And of course, I didn't have to pick it from the tall, skinny matrix.

184
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
I could have picked it from the short, wide matrix.

185
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
Or I could even just decide to average these two vectors

186
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
and use that as the embedding overall.

187
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:18,000
And there's actually a good reason to assume that these vectors

188
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
would represent a decent amount of meaning.

189
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
The reason is that an entry in the big co-occurrence matrix

190
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
is equivalent to the dot product of a word vector

191
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
taken from the tall, skinny matrix

192
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and a word vector taken from the wide, short matrix.

193
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,000
So basically, a given co-occurrence

194
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:42,000
is approximated by a dot product between two word vectors, basically.

195
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
So if I use these word vectors,

196
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
what it tells me is that now the dot product represents

197
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
how likely two words are to co-occur.

198
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
So I've gotten this structure in my vectors.

199
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Correlation in vectors corresponds to correlation in context.

200
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
So that is why you might expect matrix decompositions

201
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
to give you good embeddings.

202
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
So now I'll tell you a little bit about the particular method

203
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
that I used to generate the word embeddings

204
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
at the beginning of this video.

205
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
The method I used is known as glove,

206
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
which is short for global vectors.

207
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
And it is a kind of co-occurrence decomposition method.

208
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Now, it's a little unique in that it decomposes the log,

209
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
the logarithm of the co-occurrence matrix

210
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
instead of the actual co-occurrence matrix.

211
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,000
And it also is weighted.

212
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:34,000
It uses a model where certain entries in the co-occurrence matrix

213
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
are more important than others.

214
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,000
And you use gradient descent to learn the embeddings,

215
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
so it's similar to training a neural network.

216
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
And it has really good results, and it's extremely fast.

217
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
So I really liked glove.

218
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
I had a lot more fun implementing glove

219
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
than I did implementing word divac.

220
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
And I will certainly have a link to that paper

221
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
in the description that describes glove,

222
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
because it's an excellent paper.

223
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
It explains why word divac works as well as why glove works.

224
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
And it talks about a bunch of other word embedding methods.

225
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
So that's pretty much all I had planned for today.

226
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:11,000
I hope I got you really interested in word embeddings.

227
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
And if you want to know more,

228
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
